BACKGROUND = [
    {"id": "bg-index", "after": "section-1-p2", "title": "The index as a twisted partition function", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A supersymmetric theory has a conserved fermion number $(-1)^F$ and supercharges that pair every state of non-zero energy with a partner of opposite statistics. In the trace $\operatorname{Tr}\,e^{-\beta H}(-1)^F$ such pairs cancel, so only states annihilated by some supercharges, the BPS states, contribute, and the result does not change with $\beta$ or with any continuous coupling. For strings on $\mathbb R^d$ the relevant object is a $\tfrac12$-BPS index that counts states with given momentum and winding charges; it can be computed at zero string coupling and gives $S_\text{micro}=4\pi\sqrt{|Q_nQ_w|}$. The paper's observation is that this index is also a point in a continuous family: since rotating by $2\pi$ gives $(-1)^F=e^{2\pi iJ}$, inserting $e^{-\beta\Omega J}$ with $\beta\Omega=2\pi i$ into the thermal partition function produces exactly the index, so one can turn the angular velocity on gradually and watch each saddle of the thermal problem move towards $\nu=0$.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-hagedorn", "after": "section-1-p3", "title": "The thermal circle, the winding tachyon and the Atick–Witten theory", "audience": ["student"],
     "body": r"""<p>A thermal partition function is a Euclidean path integral with time compactified on a circle of circumference $\beta=2\pi R$. For strings there is a new kind of state: a closed string wound once around this circle, with mass $m^2\sim R^2-R_H^2$ in string units. Above the Hagedorn temperature ($R<R_H$) it is tachyonic, which is the Euclidean face of the exponential growth of string states. Just below, $R-R_H\ll1$, it is a light complex scalar, the thermal scalar, and together with the massless supergravity fields it forms the Atick–Witten effective theory in one dimension less. Horowitz and Polchinski used it to find a ball of winding condensate held together by its own gravity, the string star of this paper's title.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-enhancement", "after": "subsec-worldsheet-p5", "title": "Gauge enhancement at the self-dual radius", "audience": ["student", "physicist"],
     "body": r"""<p>A heterotic string on a circle of radius $R$ has two $U(1)$ gauge fields from the circle, the Kaluza–Klein vector and the $B$-field vector, or in a chiral basis $U(1)_L\times U(1)_R$, with the radius itself appearing as a massless scalar. At the self-dual radius $R=1$ ($\alpha'=1$), the states with one unit of momentum and one unit of winding become massless vectors with charge $\pm1$ under $U(1)_L$, and $U(1)_L$ is enhanced to $SU(2)_L$. Moving away from $R=1$ is a Higgs mechanism: the radius is the vacuum value of an adjoint scalar, the two charged vectors eat two scalars and become a complex massive W boson with $m_W^2=(R-1/R)^2$. The W boson is therefore the string with winding $\pm1$, which is exactly the thermal winding mode when the circle is the thermal one.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-hp", "after": "sec-w_stars-p1", "title": "The Horowitz–Polchinski string star and the correspondence principle", "audience": ["physicist", "student"],
     "body": r"""<p>Susskind, and Horowitz and Polchinski, proposed that a black hole shrinking by Hawking evaporation becomes, when its horizon reaches the string scale, a single long excited string: at that point the black hole entropy $S\sim r_h^{D-2}/G_N$ and the string entropy $S\sim l_sE$ match, up to order-one factors, for $g_s^2S\sim1$. Horowitz and Polchinski later found the self-gravitating string state as a solution of the Atick–Witten theory: the light thermal scalar sources a Newtonian potential through a cubic coupling, and in $2<d<6$ spatial dimensions the attraction supports a normalisable condensate of size $L\sim1/m$ and action $I\sim m^{6-d}/G_N$. Whether this solution is continuously connected to the black hole is the question this paper examines near the index.</p>"""},
    {"id": "bg-two-charge", "after": "subsec-charged_setup-p1", "title": "Two-charge BPS states and the two-charge black hole", "audience": ["student", "physicist"],
     "body": r"""<p>A heterotic string wound $Q_w$ times around a circle and carrying $Q_n$ units of momentum along it can be BPS when only its left-moving oscillators are excited; the number of such states grows as $e^{4\pi\sqrt{|Q_nQ_w|}}$ (Dabholkar and Harvey), and this count is what the index gives. On the gravity side the same charges are carried by a two-charge black hole whose extremal limit has vanishing horizon area in supergravity, the small black hole. Whether higher-derivative ($\alpha'$) corrections give it a finite area equal to the microscopic entropy has been argued since the 1990s, and the recent proposals of a BPS saddle for the gravitational index belong to the same debate.</p>"""},
]
